; Host in the loop: the program does the work, and YOU decide when it is done.
;
; `(& port body)` is the only way a value reaches a program from outside it, and
; `std/seam` gives that pattern its names. Here it is the loop's stop rule. Each
; round the run pauses in an AWAITING INPUT state — a healthy pause, not a
; failure, like a program waiting for a message — and you send `1` to accept the
; draft or `0` to send it round again.
;
; The verdict costs no model call. A port used where a value flows just hands
; over what the host delivered. That is the whole reason to gate on a port
; instead of on a judge prompt: a model asked "is this good enough?" is reading
; the very answer it is judging and can be talked round by it. You cannot.
;
; WHERE THE VERDICT GOES. In the terminal: `/run`, then `/runs`, select the
; awaiting run and press Enter — that opens an input line, and Enter delivers the
; value to the port and resumes the run where it stopped. That is the only way in.
; `/record FILE` does NOT feed a port: a record is evidence for scoring, and it
; reaches the run as its record, not as an input.
;
; In the visual editor: run it, then open Runs. A waiting run shows the port it
; is stopped on with a box beside it; type the value and press Enter or click
; send. Its RECORD / INPUT box is the record, the same as above — not a port.
;
; One constraint there: a DRY run is evaluated without an input seam at all, so
; ports only work in the direct or chaos modes. A dry run reports the port
; unavailable instead of waiting for you.
;
; With nothing wired at all the port is unbound, the gate sees neither 0 nor 1,
; and the run stops on an InvalidCondition after a single call — it cannot spin.
; A loop gated by a port is only as alive as its host.
;
; THE COST, MEASURED. Three rounds is three verdicts from you and nine model
; calls — not three. `std-loop` splices its value into both the stop test and the
; continuation (std/loops documents this), so every round re-runs the whole chain
; that produced the draft. The language has no variables, so the loop cannot hold
; the draft between the test and the branch. Budget for that before putting
; expensive work inside a loop.
;
; If you do not need to SEE each draft before deciding, gate on the port before
; the work instead — `(& seam (% seam done (again …)))` — and three rounds costs
; three calls. You are then choosing how many rounds to run, not judging what
; each one produced.

(# std/loops)
(# std/seam)

; One round of work.
(~ tighten (draft)
  (-> draft
      "Tighten this by one pass: same meaning, fewer words, nothing lost."))

; The stop rule is you. `std-received` runs the work and then takes your
; delivered value as its answer, so you see the draft before you reply.
(~ accepted (draft)
  (std-received draft))

(std-loop
  "Write one plain paragraph explaining what an input port is for."
  tighten
  accepted)
